This is closely related to #279, #561 (and possibly subsumed there), but wanted to highlight it.

Consider:

```r
# A
for (i in x) {
  if (i > 5) {
    do_something()
  }
}

# vs

# B
for (i in x) {
  if (i <= 5) next
  do_something()
}
```

These two loops are logically identical, but if we never have a test case where `any(x <= 5)`, A can generate 100% coverage while B is 75% (as the `next` is not run).

The way to square this is to spell out the implicit `else` clause:

```r
for (i in x) {
  if (i > 5) {
    do_something()
  } else NULL
}
```

The trouble is with how to report this -- "coverage" is always reported as "this line is tested, this one is not", so conveying "this line is tested, but the implicit alternative is not" does not map well to web interfaces where we consume coverage as "red line, green line".

Probably, there's nothing we can do here, but it is a surprise when refactoring code like A with 100% coverage into code like B and coverage drops.

This can be reproduced with : 

package code : 

```
foo <- function(cond) {
  if(cond) {
    print("TRUE!") }
  else  {
    print("FALSE!")
  }
}
```

tests :

```
test_that("foo works", {
  expect_snapshot({
    foo(TRUE)
  })

  expect_snapshot({
    foo(FALSE)
  })
})
```

output : 

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/18351714/122743323-2fd77780-d287-11eb-975d-3528b66586f7.png)


The following would work fine : 

```
test_that("foo works", {
  expect_snapshot({
    foo(TRUE)
    foo(FALSE)
  })
})
```

Thanks for the great work.

Hi,

I seem to have discovered an edge case where function coverage is not detected correctly.

Let's first start with a slightly simpler case that actually works:
```r
# covr_test.R
##################################
some_env <- environment()

def <- function(fn) {
  some_env$global_fun <- fn
}

def(
  fn =
    function(x) {
      x ^ 2
    }
)

get_global_fn <- function() {
  some_env$global_fun
}

exec <- function(arg) {
  get_global_fn()(arg)
}

# covr_test_file.R
##################################
library(testthat)

test_that("blah", {
  expect_equal(exec(5), 5^2)
})
```
Attention should go the function that is defined by `def`, i.e. the square function.
The test indirectly calls this function and this should also be reported by covr.
```r
> as.data.frame(file_coverage("covr_test.R", "covr_test_file.R"))
Test passed 😀
